  "narrative": "The H115 validation response resolves the low/medium-serum TP53-loss boundary. The signed TP53-loss effect among lines with media_serum_pct <= 5 was +0.335 [0.176, 0.495] in discovery (R61) and +0.441 [0.239, 0.643] in independent validation (R65). Both estimates are in the claimed higher-dependency_composite direction and both lower bounds exceed the 0.15 composite-score-unit threshold. I therefore accept H115 and close it: TP53 loss is dataset-specifically associated with higher dependency_composite scores, i.e. weaker measured dependency, in the <=5% serum context. This reinforces the accepted central pattern and does not establish causality or generalizability. The H157 validation is the more informative new result for the unresolved CDKN2A boundary. CDKN2A loss in copy_number_9p21 <= -0.5 had signed discovery estimate +0.157 [0.022, 0.292] (R90) and independent validation estimate +0.220 [0.019, 0.420] (R92), where positive is the claimed stronger-dependency/lower-score direction. The validation point estimate is above threshold and both intervals exclude zero, but the validation lower bound is only 0.019, far below the 0.15 practical threshold, and the validation comparator cell is small (n=115). Thus the effect remains directionally consistent but is not supported as a threshold-robust scientifically meaningful difference. I therefore keep H157 unresolved and close this CDKN2A context after validation. This is a negative finding for threshold-level practical importance in this subgroup, not proof that no small association exists. The broader supported scientific synthesis remains unchanged: within the supplied dataset, KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss are associated with higher dependency_composite scores and weaker measured dependency, while SMARCA4 loss is associated with lower scores and stronger measured dependency. These central associations are validated overall and replicated across many contexts, including adenocarcinoma, serum contexts, high expression states, missing RNA-omics contexts, copy-number loss/gain states, high screen-quality subsets, and several co-alteration backgrounds. Negative findings remain important: research markers D/E/F, MTAP main effect, KRAS mutation, ARID1A loss in the originally claimed stronger-dependency direction, STK11, MYC amplification, and several interaction contrasts did not show threshold-robust effects. Unresolved uncertainty remains mainly in near-threshold claims: CDKN2A loss overall and in several co-contexts, the KEAP1-by-TP53-loss interaction H50, and unanalyzed exact-context or genomic-instability hypotheses. The most informative next analyses are the already registered genomic-instability interactions for KEAP1, SMARCA4, and TP53 loss with high aneuploidy, high ploidy, and high mutation burden; they test plausible context modification and can either reveal new threshold-level subgroups or further stabilize the main effects. If those are null or small, the next priority is broad screening of untested binary predictors and technical covariates. I would not prioritize further CDKN2A validation unless a new hypothesis has a much stronger point estimate, because the H157 validation shows the independent interval is too wide for the current boundary effect to be practically accepted."
}
